a Negative Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "ae30298b-d3cf-50b0-8100-8d0fc1d6a38b" :step/math-academy-id 26284 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185352 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients. \n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x+3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =-3 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding  $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =-3+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac12$ and $x=\\dfrac32.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d202804-803f-5139-b32a-fca689609abb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9c761f1-c1eb-55db-b51b-2f4b7c894767" :step/math-academy-id 37719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183914 :question/id #uuid "9815dede-5c47-5430-a58a-218313f78412" :question/math-academy-id "e-10014"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"} :step/id #uuid "4923c949-f86d-5707-8ac7-0bcd1b408f05" :step/math-academy-id 37726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185300 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc56cc55-b423-5e0b-aed0-e07bdb86f874" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13276 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Formula"} :step/id #uuid "3f1ff0dc-f8c4-5e2b-90b2-8f6efe37b016" :step/math-academy-id 461792}] :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Completing the Square" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184433}]}
